1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,334
Somewhere in America,

2
00:00:02,334 --> 00:00:04,785
a robot company is planning a giant

3
00:00:04,785 --> 00:00:05,877
new factory,

4
00:00:05,877 --> 00:00:08,260
a place where robots build robots.

5
00:00:08,440 --> 00:00:09,680
Meet Gus.

6
00:00:09,680 --> 00:00:11,206
He's the factory scout,

7
00:00:11,206 --> 00:00:12,976
and his boss has given him a

8
00:00:12,976 --> 00:00:14,110
single job.

9
00:00:14,110 --> 00:00:15,712
Find the best spot on Earth to

10
00:00:15,712 --> 00:00:16,770
build it.

11
00:00:17,020 --> 00:00:17,912
For decades,

12
00:00:17,912 --> 00:00:20,150
that question had an easy answer.

13
00:00:20,280 --> 00:00:22,210
Go where workers are cheap.

14
00:00:22,210 --> 00:00:24,577
But Gus's new factory barely needs

15
00:00:24,577 --> 00:00:25,490
workers.

16
00:00:25,490 --> 00:00:27,360
So the answer changes.

17
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:29,600
And a country that never looked like

18
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:31,960
the obvious pick suddenly does.

19
00:00:32,090 --> 00:00:34,026
Could the robot in your future be

20
00:00:34,026 --> 00:00:35,400
made in Brazil?

21
00:00:35,850 --> 00:00:42,767
If you like finding the hidden money behind the headlines, hit subscribe and like. It really helps the channel.

22
00:00:58,417 --> 00:01:01,467
Gus works for Boltwell Robotics.

23
00:01:01,527 --> 00:01:02,917
His boss spins a big globe.

24
00:01:02,917 --> 00:01:05,793
These robots work all night,

25
00:01:05,793 --> 00:01:07,167
she says.

26
00:01:07,167 --> 00:01:08,552
They never get tired,

27
00:01:08,552 --> 00:01:10,747
and they need very few people.

28
00:01:10,747 --> 00:01:13,157
So stop looking for cheap workers.

29
00:01:13,157 --> 00:01:14,124
Look for cheap,

30
00:01:14,124 --> 00:01:15,407
clean power.

31
00:01:15,407 --> 00:01:17,189
Look for the metals robots are made

32
00:01:17,189 --> 00:01:17,907
of.

33
00:01:17,907 --> 00:01:21,277
And look for lots of customers.

34
00:01:21,277 --> 00:01:22,047
Gus nods.

35
00:01:22,047 --> 00:01:22,316
So,

36
00:01:22,316 --> 00:01:22,937
where?

37
00:01:22,937 --> 00:01:24,447
The boss smiles.

38
00:01:24,447 --> 00:01:26,182
That's your job.

39
00:01:26,417 --> 00:01:28,897
Let me draw what changes.

40
00:01:29,017 --> 00:01:31,367
Here's the bill for making a robot.

41
00:01:31,367 --> 00:01:32,831
In an old factory,

42
00:01:32,831 --> 00:01:34,506
the heaviest part was wages,

43
00:01:34,506 --> 00:01:36,087
people on the line.

44
00:01:36,087 --> 00:01:38,343
In a factory full of robots,

45
00:01:38,343 --> 00:01:39,477
that part shrinks.

46
00:01:39,477 --> 00:01:40,817
So what's left?

47
00:01:40,817 --> 00:01:43,307
The power that runs the machines.

48
00:01:43,307 --> 00:01:44,927
The metal they're made of.

49
00:01:44,927 --> 00:01:47,187
The cost of moving things around.

50
00:01:47,187 --> 00:01:48,678
And the cost of the money you

51
00:01:48,678 --> 00:01:50,397
borrow to build it all.

52
00:01:50,527 --> 00:01:53,246
Whoever makes those cheapest wins the next

53
00:01:53,246 --> 00:01:54,662
factory.

54
00:01:54,917 --> 00:01:57,457
Then Gus gets a video call.

55
00:01:57,457 --> 00:01:58,128
It's Bia,

56
00:01:58,128 --> 00:02:00,417
an engineer from Minas Gerais,

57
00:02:00,417 --> 00:02:02,867
a big mining state in Brazil.

58
00:02:02,997 --> 00:02:04,498
She's read about the project,

59
00:02:04,498 --> 00:02:06,207
and she wants that factory in her

60
00:02:06,207 --> 00:02:07,187
home state.

61
00:02:07,257 --> 00:02:07,997
Come and see,

62
00:02:07,997 --> 00:02:08,747
she says.

63
00:02:08,747 --> 00:02:09,790
We have the power,

64
00:02:09,790 --> 00:02:11,597
the metals and the people.

65
00:02:11,597 --> 00:02:13,406
Gus grabs a little backpack for the

66
00:02:13,406 --> 00:02:14,067
trip.

67
00:02:14,067 --> 00:02:15,307
Bia laughs.

68
00:02:15,307 --> 00:02:16,377
Pack light.

69
00:02:16,377 --> 00:02:17,820
You'll see why.

70
00:02:22,284 --> 00:02:23,069
First stop,

71
00:02:23,069 --> 00:02:25,477
a giant dam on a wide river,

72
00:02:25,477 --> 00:02:28,183
with wind turbines on the hills behind

73
00:02:28,183 --> 00:02:29,054
it.

74
00:02:29,224 --> 00:02:30,078
In Brazil,

75
00:02:30,078 --> 00:02:32,487
almost nine out of every ten units

76
00:02:32,487 --> 00:02:35,115
of electricity come from renewable

77
00:02:35,115 --> 00:02:35,750
sources,

78
00:02:35,750 --> 00:02:37,261
mostly falling water,

79
00:02:37,261 --> 00:02:39,394
plus fast-growing wind and sun.

80
00:02:39,454 --> 00:02:41,254
Gus is impressed.

81
00:02:41,374 --> 00:02:43,466
A robot factory could run on that

82
00:02:43,466 --> 00:02:44,694
day and night.

83
00:02:44,844 --> 00:02:47,327
We told Brazil's power story in Could

84
00:02:47,327 --> 00:02:50,659
Cheap Electricity Become the New Oil?

85
00:02:50,917 --> 00:02:51,969
Second stop,

86
00:02:51,969 --> 00:02:54,677
the red hills of Minas Gerais.

87
00:02:54,677 --> 00:02:56,769
Huge trains roll out of the mines,

88
00:02:56,769 --> 00:02:58,391
loaded with iron ore,

89
00:02:58,391 --> 00:03:00,637
the rock that becomes steel.

90
00:03:00,857 --> 00:03:03,116
Brazil is the second-biggest producer of

91
00:03:03,116 --> 00:03:04,657
iron ore on Earth.

92
00:03:04,757 --> 00:03:06,549
Gus pulls up a robot arm on

93
00:03:06,549 --> 00:03:07,527
his phone.

94
00:03:07,527 --> 00:03:10,198
And robots are mostly steel.

95
00:03:10,450 --> 00:03:13,157
Then Bia hands Gus a tiny jar

96
00:03:13,157 --> 00:03:15,110
of gray powder.

97
00:03:15,290 --> 00:03:17,160
This is niobium.

98
00:03:17,180 --> 00:03:19,020
A pinch of it makes steel much

99
00:03:19,020 --> 00:03:20,770
stronger and lighter.

100
00:03:20,910 --> 00:03:22,773
And Brazil mines more than nine out

101
00:03:22,773 --> 00:03:24,636
of every ten tons of it in

102
00:03:24,636 --> 00:03:25,650
the world.

103
00:03:25,890 --> 00:03:27,810
Gus nearly drops the jar.

104
00:03:27,810 --> 00:03:30,070
You have almost all of it?

105
00:03:30,130 --> 00:03:31,530
Bia grins.

106
00:03:31,530 --> 00:03:33,486
Most of it comes from a single

107
00:03:33,486 --> 00:03:34,955
mine in my state.

108
00:03:35,217 --> 00:03:37,349
Robots also need motors,

109
00:03:37,349 --> 00:03:39,777
and the best motors need magnets made

110
00:03:39,777 --> 00:03:41,187
from rare earths.

111
00:03:41,227 --> 00:03:43,210
Brazil has the second-largest known

112
00:03:43,210 --> 00:03:45,612
reserves of rare earths on the planet,

113
00:03:45,612 --> 00:03:48,014
though today it barely mines them,

114
00:03:48,014 --> 00:03:49,777
and China still does most of that

115
00:03:49,777 --> 00:03:50,597
work.

116
00:03:50,687 --> 00:03:52,817
And in the Jequitinhonha Valley,

117
00:03:52,817 --> 00:03:55,162
lithium for batteries is already being dug

118
00:03:55,162 --> 00:03:56,934
up and shipped abroad.

119
00:03:57,184 --> 00:03:59,854
Let me draw the treasure map.

120
00:04:00,064 --> 00:04:01,654
Here's Brazil.

121
00:04:01,854 --> 00:04:04,354
Rivers and wind for power.

122
00:04:04,464 --> 00:04:06,224
Iron in the hills.

123
00:04:06,224 --> 00:04:09,494
Niobium in a single mining town.

124
00:04:09,584 --> 00:04:12,684
Rare earths and lithium underground.

125
00:04:12,774 --> 00:04:13,288
Farms,

126
00:04:13,288 --> 00:04:16,324
and big cities full of customers.

127
00:04:16,414 --> 00:04:17,446
On paper,

128
00:04:17,446 --> 00:04:20,092
that's almost the whole recipe for a

129
00:04:20,092 --> 00:04:20,859
robot,

130
00:04:20,859 --> 00:04:23,312
inside a single country.

131
00:04:23,550 --> 00:04:24,422
Next,

132
00:04:24,422 --> 00:04:25,820
the farms.

133
00:04:25,820 --> 00:04:28,587
Fields of soybeans roll to the horizon,

134
00:04:28,587 --> 00:04:31,379
and Brazil is the world's biggest exporter

135
00:04:31,379 --> 00:04:32,740
of soybeans.

136
00:04:32,930 --> 00:04:36,080
Food and land are no problem here.

137
00:04:36,280 --> 00:04:38,800
Gus takes a photo for his boss.

138
00:04:38,840 --> 00:04:40,665
She texts back a whole row of

139
00:04:40,665 --> 00:04:42,405
exclamation marks.

140
00:04:42,650 --> 00:04:45,350
And there's something people outside

141
00:04:45,350 --> 00:04:47,570
Brazil often forget.

142
00:04:47,600 --> 00:04:49,535
It already knows how to build hard

143
00:04:49,535 --> 00:04:50,350
things.

144
00:04:50,380 --> 00:04:52,543
Brazilian companies make passenger jets

145
00:04:52,543 --> 00:04:54,097
that fly all over the world,

146
00:04:54,097 --> 00:04:56,260
and electric motors sold on every

147
00:04:56,260 --> 00:04:57,450
continent.

148
00:04:57,470 --> 00:04:59,615
Bia studied with engineers who work on

149
00:04:59,615 --> 00:05:00,430
them.

150
00:05:00,460 --> 00:05:02,189
We don't just dig things up,

151
00:05:02,189 --> 00:05:03,200
she says.

152
00:05:03,200 --> 00:05:05,225
We build things too.

153
00:05:05,484 --> 00:05:06,058
Last,

154
00:05:06,058 --> 00:05:07,444
the city.

155
00:05:07,444 --> 00:05:08,596
Busy streets,

156
00:05:08,596 --> 00:05:09,157
shops,

157
00:05:09,157 --> 00:05:10,013
traffic,

158
00:05:10,013 --> 00:05:11,204
music.

159
00:05:11,234 --> 00:05:13,296
More than two hundred million people live

160
00:05:13,296 --> 00:05:14,101
in Brazil,

161
00:05:14,101 --> 00:05:16,782
a huge market for everything a factory

162
00:05:16,782 --> 00:05:17,784
makes.

163
00:05:17,784 --> 00:05:19,904
Gus counts on his fingers.

164
00:05:19,904 --> 00:05:20,751
Power,

165
00:05:20,751 --> 00:05:21,597
metal,

166
00:05:21,597 --> 00:05:22,444
magnets,

167
00:05:22,444 --> 00:05:22,998
food,

168
00:05:22,998 --> 00:05:24,474
customers.

169
00:05:24,544 --> 00:05:26,201
So why isn't everyone building here

170
00:05:26,201 --> 00:05:27,354
already?

171
00:05:27,564 --> 00:05:29,044
Bia sighs.

172
00:05:29,044 --> 00:05:31,077
Let me show you the backpack.

173
00:05:35,517 --> 00:05:36,977
Here's the twist.

174
00:05:36,977 --> 00:05:37,757
For years,

175
00:05:37,757 --> 00:05:40,178
Brazil's factories have been losing

176
00:05:40,178 --> 00:05:40,927
ground.

177
00:05:40,927 --> 00:05:42,865
Today they make only about one-eighth of

178
00:05:42,865 --> 00:05:45,187
everything the country produces.

179
00:05:45,187 --> 00:05:47,709
Economists have a long word for it,

180
00:05:47,709 --> 00:05:49,697
deindustrialization.

181
00:05:49,747 --> 00:05:51,427
Gus looks around.

182
00:05:51,427 --> 00:05:53,127
With all this treasure?

183
00:05:53,127 --> 00:05:54,417
Bia nods.

184
00:05:54,417 --> 00:05:57,088
Making things here costs extra.

185
00:05:57,350 --> 00:05:59,927
The backpack tour begins at the tax

186
00:05:59,927 --> 00:06:01,100
counter.

187
00:06:01,180 --> 00:06:02,067
In Brazil,

188
00:06:02,067 --> 00:06:04,940
the bill to produce carries extra weight.

189
00:06:05,210 --> 00:06:07,433
As a part moves from factory to

190
00:06:07,433 --> 00:06:08,301
factory,

191
00:06:08,301 --> 00:06:09,612
it picks up taxes,

192
00:06:09,612 --> 00:06:11,391
and some taxes land on top of

193
00:06:11,391 --> 00:06:12,481
other taxes,

194
00:06:12,481 --> 00:06:13,970
like a snowball.

195
00:06:14,320 --> 00:06:16,429
Even the power bill carries a heavy

196
00:06:16,429 --> 00:06:16,793
load,

197
00:06:16,793 --> 00:06:18,135
as we saw in the new oil

198
00:06:18,135 --> 00:06:19,310
episode.

199
00:06:19,680 --> 00:06:20,660
Thunk.

200
00:06:20,660 --> 00:06:23,805
The first brick lands in Gus's backpack.

201
00:06:24,050 --> 00:06:24,614
Next,

202
00:06:24,614 --> 00:06:26,400
the paperwork.

203
00:06:26,590 --> 00:06:29,045
Brazil's tax rules are famous for being

204
00:06:29,045 --> 00:06:29,890
thick.

205
00:06:29,910 --> 00:06:31,576
In the World Bank's last worldwide

206
00:06:31,576 --> 00:06:32,743
comparison,

207
00:06:32,743 --> 00:06:34,886
companies here spent more hours on tax

208
00:06:34,886 --> 00:06:37,250
forms than anywhere else on Earth.

209
00:06:37,250 --> 00:06:38,300
Thunk.

210
00:06:38,300 --> 00:06:39,610
Another brick.

211
00:06:39,700 --> 00:06:42,205
Gus leans a little to the left.

212
00:06:42,450 --> 00:06:43,239
Then,

213
00:06:43,239 --> 00:06:44,670
the road.

214
00:06:44,690 --> 00:06:46,366
Brazil is enormous,

215
00:06:46,366 --> 00:06:48,796
and about two of every three tons

216
00:06:48,796 --> 00:06:50,715
of its cargo ride on trucks,

217
00:06:50,715 --> 00:06:52,148
over long highways,

218
00:06:52,148 --> 00:06:53,720
to busy ports.

219
00:06:53,910 --> 00:06:55,631
Over distances this big,

220
00:06:55,631 --> 00:06:57,787
trains and ships are usually cheaper,

221
00:06:57,787 --> 00:07:00,130
but there are far fewer of them.

222
00:07:00,170 --> 00:07:00,960
Thunk.

223
00:07:00,960 --> 00:07:02,800
Another brick.

224
00:07:02,800 --> 00:07:04,474
Gus starts to sweat.

225
00:07:04,717 --> 00:07:06,080
And finally,

226
00:07:06,080 --> 00:07:07,237
the bank.

227
00:07:07,547 --> 00:07:08,885
To build a factory,

228
00:07:08,885 --> 00:07:09,967
you borrow.

229
00:07:09,967 --> 00:07:12,367
And Brazil's central bank keeps its key

230
00:07:12,367 --> 00:07:15,540
interest rate at almost fourteen percent,

231
00:07:15,540 --> 00:07:16,847
high on purpose,

232
00:07:16,847 --> 00:07:19,047
to keep prices in check.

233
00:07:19,197 --> 00:07:21,661
So every loan in the country costs

234
00:07:21,661 --> 00:07:22,129
more,

235
00:07:22,129 --> 00:07:24,947
for families and for factories.

236
00:07:25,047 --> 00:07:26,117
Thunk.

237
00:07:26,117 --> 00:07:28,111
Gus's knees wobble.

238
00:07:28,350 --> 00:07:29,851
Add it all up,

239
00:07:29,851 --> 00:07:32,271
and the backpack has a name,

240
00:07:32,271 --> 00:07:34,078
the Custo Brasil,

241
00:07:34,078 --> 00:07:36,210
the cost of Brazil.

242
00:07:36,380 --> 00:07:38,654
A study by a business group,

243
00:07:38,654 --> 00:07:41,387
working with Brazil's industry ministry,

244
00:07:41,387 --> 00:07:43,891
put the extra cost of producing here,

245
00:07:43,891 --> 00:07:46,624
compared with the average rich country,

246
00:07:46,624 --> 00:07:49,150
at about three hundred billion dollars a

247
00:07:49,150 --> 00:07:49,980
year.

248
00:07:50,300 --> 00:07:51,593
That's an estimate,

249
00:07:51,593 --> 00:07:53,070
but it's heavy.

250
00:07:53,330 --> 00:07:55,970
Gus sits down on his backpack.

251
00:07:55,970 --> 00:07:56,570
Or,

252
00:07:56,570 --> 00:07:58,747
it sits down on him.

253
00:07:58,984 --> 00:08:01,644
Now here's the catch for robots.

254
00:08:01,644 --> 00:08:02,984
Let me draw it.

255
00:08:03,084 --> 00:08:05,273
Robots take the wage brick out of

256
00:08:05,273 --> 00:08:06,694
the backpack.

257
00:08:06,854 --> 00:08:09,256
But they don't touch the tax brick,

258
00:08:09,256 --> 00:08:11,984
the truck brick or the loan brick.

259
00:08:12,184 --> 00:08:15,056
And a robot factory is mostly expensive

260
00:08:15,056 --> 00:08:15,918
machines,

261
00:08:15,918 --> 00:08:18,064
bought with borrowed money.

262
00:08:18,144 --> 00:08:20,388
So in a robot factory,

263
00:08:20,388 --> 00:08:22,128
the loan brick gets heavier,

264
00:08:22,128 --> 00:08:23,484
not lighter.

265
00:08:23,804 --> 00:08:26,057
Robots make Brazil's treasure more

266
00:08:26,057 --> 00:08:27,156
valuable,

267
00:08:27,156 --> 00:08:29,728
and its backpack more painful.

268
00:08:34,184 --> 00:08:35,696
The good news is,

269
00:08:35,696 --> 00:08:38,694
Brazil knows about the backpack.

270
00:08:38,694 --> 00:08:40,905
It recently passed the biggest change to

271
00:08:40,905 --> 00:08:43,314
its sales taxes in decades.

272
00:08:43,324 --> 00:08:45,321
A pile of old taxes is being

273
00:08:45,321 --> 00:08:47,519
replaced by a simpler system,

274
00:08:47,519 --> 00:08:49,516
built so that taxes stop landing on

275
00:08:49,516 --> 00:08:51,004
top of taxes.

276
00:08:51,064 --> 00:08:53,374
Bia pulls the first brick out.

277
00:08:53,374 --> 00:08:54,014
See?

278
00:08:54,014 --> 00:08:56,013
It's already a little lighter.

279
00:08:56,250 --> 00:08:57,513
The catch is,

280
00:08:57,513 --> 00:08:58,690
it's slow.

281
00:08:58,690 --> 00:09:00,668
The new system started its test run

282
00:09:00,668 --> 00:09:01,305
this year,

283
00:09:01,305 --> 00:09:03,722
and the old taxes don't fully disappear

284
00:09:03,722 --> 00:09:05,630
until twenty thirty-three.

285
00:09:05,630 --> 00:09:07,570
Gus checks his calendar.

286
00:09:07,590 --> 00:09:09,465
That's a long time to carry a

287
00:09:09,465 --> 00:09:10,534
backpack.

288
00:09:10,784 --> 00:09:13,304
And factories can come back.

289
00:09:13,474 --> 00:09:14,411
In Bahia,

290
00:09:14,411 --> 00:09:16,333
Ford closed its giant car plant in

291
00:09:16,333 --> 00:09:17,844
Camaçari and left.

292
00:09:17,844 --> 00:09:19,014
The halls went quiet.

293
00:09:19,054 --> 00:09:21,586
Then a Chinese carmaker,

294
00:09:21,586 --> 00:09:22,195
BYD,

295
00:09:22,195 --> 00:09:24,087
took over the site,

296
00:09:24,087 --> 00:09:27,292
